TITLE:: NdefRackDisplay
summary:: the live picture in a scope or a spectrum module, and over a cable
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRackModuleView, Classes/NdefRackLib, Classes/FFT, Classes/BufWr

DESCRIPTION::
A scope or spectrum module is an Ndef that passes its input straight through to
its own bus. The picture is not the Ndef's business: the window gives the module
one of these, which runs a small synth of its own reading that bus into a
link::Classes/Buffer::, and reads the Buffer back from the language whenever the
window asks.

code::
s.boot;
NdefRack.make(\wave, \sine).set(\freq, 220);
NdefRack.make(\scope, \sc);
NdefRack.connect(\sine, \sc, \in);

d = NdefRackDisplay(\scope, NdefRack.at(\sc));
d.poll({ d.data.collect(_.size).postln });    // two traces, 512 values each
d.free;
::

You do not usually make these: link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView:: makes one for
every module whose library entry says code::display::, polls it at every frame
of the window and frees it with the view; and link::Classes/NdefRackGUI:: makes
a code::\probe:: for the audio cable whose picture is open, polls it while it
stays open and frees it a second after it closes - at once when its module
goes.

subsection:: What it shows

table::
## strong::kind:: || strong::the synth:: || strong::data::
## code::\scope:: || writes code::frames:: samples of every channel from the moment channel 1 rises through zero, code::time:: seconds wide; a capture runs to the end before the next trigger counts, and with no crossing for a quarter of a second it runs free || one Array of code::frames:: values per channel
## code::\spectrum:: || a Hann-windowed 2048-point code::FFT:: of the first channel, into the Buffer || dB per bin from bin 1, a full scale sine at 0 dB; each value falls by at most 1.5 dB a reading, so a peak can be read
## code::\probe:: || the scope's capture, shorter: code::probeFrames:: samples over code::probeTime:: seconds, of every channel of any audio rate module, up to 16 || one Array of code::probeFrames:: values per channel
::

The module's controls set the picture, not the sound: code::time:: and code::gain::
for the scope, code::floor:: for the spectrum - read from the module at every
poll. A probe reads no controls: its width is code::probeTime::, its gain 1, and the
window scales each trace to fill its row.

subsection:: What it leaves alone

Nothing here touches the Ndef: no node map entry, nothing in the code export,
nothing in a patch. The synth reads with code::InFeedback::, so where the Ndef
sits in the node tree does not matter, and it sits at the tail of the root node.

A boot gives it a new buffer - the old one went with the old server - and the
tree being rebuilt, after a boot or after cmd + period, a new synth. Starting
sends a SynthDef and waits for the server in a routine of its own, never in the
caller's, so a window that makes one from its frame does not stop to wait; a
display freed before the server answered starts nothing. The reads
are plain code::/b_getn::, so a remote server works too, only slower to answer.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: kind
code::\scope::, code::\spectrum:: or code::\probe::.
ARGUMENT:: module
the link::Classes/NdefRackModule:: whose output it pictures.

METHOD:: kinds
The kinds a library entry can ask for with code::display:: - code::\scope:: and
code::\spectrum::; a probe is the window's own.

METHOD:: frames
How many samples a scope trace holds, 512.

METHOD:: fftSize
The spectrum's FFT size, 2048.

METHOD:: probeFrames
How many samples a probe trace holds, 128.

METHOD:: probeTime
How many seconds a probe trace spans, 0.03. Settable; a running probe follows at
its next poll.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: poll
Ask the server for the latest picture. One request at a time; one lost on the
way is given up after a second.
ARGUMENT:: action
called with the display, from the OSC thread, once link::#-data:: holds it.

METHOD:: data
The latest picture, as the table above says; nil before the first.

METHOD:: isRunning
Whether its synth is running.

METHOD:: channels
How many traces it reads: the module's channels, up to 8 for a scope and 16 for
a probe; 1 for a spectrum.

METHOD:: time
METHOD:: gain
The scope's width in seconds and its vertical zoom, from the module's controls; a
probe's are code::probeTime:: and 1.

METHOD:: floor
The spectrum's bottom, in dB, from the module's control.

METHOD:: binFreq
The frequency of entry i of a spectrum's link::#-data::.

METHOD:: kind
METHOD:: module
METHOD:: server
What it was made with.

METHOD:: free
Stop the synth, free the Buffer, and stop following the server.
